<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mississippi Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1717">1717</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Company of the West</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> Company was a French trading company founded in 1717 as the Company of the West and enlarged in 1719 into the Company of the Indies under the financier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-law">John Law</a>. It held a monopoly of trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, recruited settlers and enslaved Africans for the colony, and was tied to Law's Banque Royale. Speculation in its shares created the Mississippi Bubble, which burst in 1720; the company returned Louisiana to the crown in 1731.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mississippi-company.jpg" alt="Mississippi Company" width="320" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frank Schulenburg · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Armoiries_de_la_Compagnie_des_Indes_Orientales.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
